{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791133940,"nonce":"6ghj0AUirDDxPPHdOMmO3w4m13Y=","title":"Plugin string results straight into the caller's string","message":"A plugin function that returns a string, called in place from a Zyntax program, allocates three times:\n1. the plugin's own Rust string;\n2. the core string `Text::new` asks the host for;\n3. the Zyntax string `$Host$text_to_string` copies it into.\n\nbenches/native.rs puts that row at about 85ns, against about 2ns for a scalar call.\n\nA host entry that lets the plugin write its bytes into a string of the caller's own kind would remove the middle copy. For example, `host::text_with(len, |buf| ...)` could write into a buffer that the calling language provides: a Zyntax string for a bound call, a core string otherwise. The ABI would need a way for the bound call to say which allocator a result's string comes from.","files":null}]}